{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n[rebar:2041-75d6-2a2f-4be9] adds an opt-in embedding retrieval tier whose vector build is an\nexplicit `build_vectors(tracker, *, limit=None)` in `src/rebar/similar/embedding.py`. That\ndesign is deliberate: `find_similar` calls `catch_up` inline\n(`src/rebar/similar/__init__.py:22`) and the create advisory calls `find_similar`\n(`src/rebar/_commands/recent_creates.py:196`), so anything catch-up does runs inside a user's\n`rebar create`. A digest-and-embed pass there would put one LLM call plus one embed per ticket\non the create path.\n\nThe consequence is that nothing invokes `build_vectors` today. Until an operator calls it from\nPython, every live entry is unvectored, coverage is never full, and `rank` returns `None` on\nevery call — the tier permanently degrades to lexical. The capability ships unreachable from\nany supported surface.\n\n## Decision: a `rebar store build-vectors` member\n\n`rebar store` is already the storage-maintenance family — \"Check, repair, measure, migrate, and\ncompact the ticket store\" (`src/rebar/_cli/_parsers/advanced/store.py`), with members `fsck`,\n`fsck-recover`, `doctor`, `maintenance`, `footprint`, `export`, `import`, `compact`\n(`src/rebar/_cli/_registry.py:453-517`). Building the similar-ticket index is maintenance of\nthat same store, so the member belongs there.\n\nRejected: a new top-level `rebar similar` namespace. One command does not justify a new\ntop-level spelling, and `src/rebar/_cli/_registry.py` registers no `similar` route today.\n\nRejected: a periodic sweep hung off an existing path. `compact --all` sets the precedent that\nthe *command* is rebar's portable surface and the schedule is the operator's choice, made\noutside rebar. A sweep that fires on its own would also have to decide when it is allowed to\nspend provider budget, which is a policy rebar has no basis to set.\n\nThe family parser auto-enumerates `STORE_ROUTES` and pulls each member's help line from the\nfirst line of that member's parser description (`advanced/store.py`, `_summary`), so adding\nthe route adds the member line to `rebar store --help` with no edit to the family parser.\n\n## Design\n\n1. **`src/rebar/similar/_cli.py`** (new) — `build_vectors_cli(argv)`. Precedent for a\n   subsystem-local CLI module is `export`/`import`, whose handlers are `rebar._io._cli:export_cli`\n   and `:import_cli` (`_registry.py:498`, `:506`).\n\n   The handler resolves configuration itself before calling the build:\n\n   - `embedding_model_name()` returns `None` → print a message naming `[llm] embedding_model`\n     and **exit 0**. The build is never entered, so no provider is contacted.\n   - otherwise call `build_vectors(tracker, limit=…)`. `status` `complete` or `partial` →\n     exit 0. `status` `unavailable` → exit 1, message carries `reason`.\n\n   Resolving \"not configured\" in the handler rather than string-matching `reason` is what keeps\n   the two meanings of `unavailable` apart: an unconfigured install is a clean no-op, a provider\n   denial is a failure the operator must see.\n\n2. **`src/rebar/_cli/_parsers/advanced/similar_build.py`** (new) — `build(*, prog)`, modelled on\n   `advanced/tracker_footprint.py`. Arguments: `--limit N` (cap the tickets vectored this run)\n   and `--output {text,json}` (default `text`), matching `footprint`'s `--output` spelling.\n\n3. **`src/rebar/_cli/_registry.py`** — a `Route(\"build-vectors\", group=\"store\", …)` appended to\n   `_store_routes()` (`:444`), with `parser_factory=\"rebar._cli._parsers.advanced.similar_build:build\"`,\n   `handler=\"rebar.similar._cli:build_vectors_cli\"`, `adapter=\"argv\"`, `init=\"full\"`. `init=\"full\"`\n   matches `maintenance` and `compact` (`:483`, `:516`): the build reads the index and the ticket\n   event log, so it needs a mounted tracker.\n\n4. **`src/rebar/_cli/_parsers/advanced/store.py`** — amend the family description to name the\n   index build, since \"check, repair, measure, migrate, and compact\" does not cover it.\n\n5. **Generated artifacts** — regenerate `src/rebar/_cli/help/store.txt` with\n   `python scripts/gen_cli_help.py` and `docs/cli-reference.md` with\n   `python scripts/gen_cli_reference.py`. Both are drift-gated in CI.\n\n6. **`tests/unit/test_similar_build_cli.py`** (new) — the ACs below.\n\n## Portability\n\nThe surface added is a CLI command plus the already-public library entry point `build_vectors`.\nNeither is tied to a CI system, and no capability here is reachable only through one. An install\nwith no provider configured runs the command to a clean exit 0 and keeps serving lexical\nresults, so the tier stays opt-in rather than becoming a dependency.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar store build-vectors --help` exits 0 and its usage line reads `Usage: rebar store build-vectors`.\n- [ ] `rebar store --help` lists `build-vectors` in the `commands` block.\n- [ ] With no `[llm] embedding_model` set, the command exits 0, its output names `embedding_model`, and an embedding stub that raises on call is never invoked.\n- [ ] With a stub embedding model configured over a tracker holding N live tickets, the command exits 0 and reports `vectored` = N; a second run reports `vectored` = 0.\n- [ ] `--limit 1` over a corpus of 2 or more uncovered tickets exits 0 and reports `status` `partial` with `remaining` ≥ 1.\n- [ ] `--output json` writes exactly one JSON object to stdout carrying `status` and all 5 counters from `_new_stats` (`vectored`, `skipped`, `empties`, `digest_unparsed`, `digest_failed`).\n- [ ] An embedding provider that raises exits 1 and the message carries the failure reason.\n- [ ] `rebar create` with an embedding stub that raises on call succeeds — the create path makes no provider call.\n- [ ] `python scripts/gen_cli_help.py --check` exits 0 against the committed artifacts.\n- [ ] `python scripts/gen_cli_reference.py --check` exits 0 against the committed artifacts.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exits 0.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the full `make verify` pass.\n\n## Testing\n\nEvery acceptance criterion above is proved by a named command, and each of the 12 maps\nto one test in the new `tests/unit/test_similar_build_cli.py` unless noted:\n\n- The `--help` criteria are proved by invoking the parser in-process and asserting on the\n  captured usage text, so they need no subprocess.\n- The counter, `--limit`, and `--output json` criteria run the command against a temporary\n  tracker seeded with live ticket dirs and a stub embedding provider, so no network call is\n  made and the assertions are on returned counters rather than on log text.\n- The provider-raises criterion asserts the non-zero exit and that the reason survives into\n  the message, rather than asserting an exact string.\n- The `rebar create` criterion lives with the create-path tests, where the stub that raises\n  on call is already the established fixture shape.\n- The three generator criteria (`gen_cli_help.py --check`, `gen_cli_reference.py --check`,\n  `check_mechanism_delta.py --check`) are repo-level gates run as commands, not as unit\n  tests; `make lint` already invokes the mechanism-delta one.\n\nFull proof before push is `make verify` from the implementation worktree, which is the\nlocally checkable half of CI's `Verified` vote.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nScheduling the build (cron, launchd, a CI job) — the operator's choice, outside rebar.\nChanging `rank`'s degrade contract or the create path's behaviour, both fixed by\n[rebar:2041-75d6-2a2f-4be9]."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793633433138840001,"uuid":"756a0efa-427c-4fb9-8885-ee5a7b2a8143"}